Relevant columns & their potential usage  
• "week": date of the trend; filter to 2024‑09‑01 – 2024‑09‑14 and Monday‑Friday only.  
• "rank": keeps the position of the term; filter to 1‑3.  
• "term": the actual query we want to inspect.  
• "refresh_date": snapshot date; will be used for GROUP BY and ORDER BY.  
• (optional) "dma_id"/"dma_name": may be used to limit to specific markets, but not required for this task.  

Typical conditions that may be applied  
• "week" BETWEEN '2024‑09‑01' AND '2024‑09‑14'  
• "rank" BETWEEN 1 AND 3  
• DAYOFWEEK("week") BETWEEN 2 AND 6   -- Monday(2) .. Friday(6) in Snowflake  
• GROUP BY "refresh_date"  
• ORDER BY "refresh_date" DESC  

Below are ten exploratory SQL statements (each limited to 20 rows) that get progressively closer to the final objective.

```sql
-- 1. Description: Peek at the raw rows inside TOP_TERMS during the target two‑week window.
SELECT *
FROM "GOOGLE_TRENDS"."GOOGLE_TRENDS"."TOP_TERMS"
WHERE "week" BETWEEN '2024-09-01' AND '2024-09-14'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Description: Inspect only the columns we eventually care about, still unrestricted on rank/weekday.
SELECT "week", "refresh_date", "term", "rank"
FROM "GOOGLE_TRENDS"."GOOGLE_TRENDS"."TOP_TERMS"
WHERE "week" BETWEEN '2024-09-01' AND '2024-09-14'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Description: Filter to top three ranks (1‑3) in the same date window.
SELECT "week", "refresh_date", "term", "rank"
FROM "GOOGLE_TRENDS"."GOOGLE_TRENDS"."TOP_TERMS"
WHERE "week" BETWEEN '2024-09-01' AND '2024-09-14'
  AND "rank" BETWEEN 1 AND 3
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Description: Add a weekday filter (Monday=2 … Friday=6) using DAYOFWEEK.
SELECT "week", "refresh_date", "term", "rank"
FROM "GOOGLE_TRENDS"."GOOGLE_TRENDS"."TOP_TERMS"
WHERE "week" BETWEEN '2024-09-01' AND '2024-09-14'
  AND "rank" BETWEEN 1 AND 3
  AND DAYOFWEEK("week") BETWEEN 2 AND 6
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Description: Show distinct refresh_date values that exist in the filtered set.
SELECT DISTINCT "refresh_date"
FROM "GOOGLE_TRENDS"."GOOGLE_TRENDS"."TOP_TERMS"
WHERE "week" BETWEEN '2024-09-01' AND '2024-09-14'
  AND "rank" BETWEEN 1 AND 3
  AND DAYOFWEEK("week") BETWEEN 2 AND 6
ORDER BY "refresh_date" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Description: Count how many qualifying terms exist per refresh_date.
SELECT "refresh_date",
       COUNT(*) AS "num_terms_rank_1_to_3"
FROM "GOOGLE_TRENDS"."GOOGLE_TRENDS"."TOP_TERMS"
WHERE "week" BETWEEN '2024-09-01' AND '2024-09-14'
  AND "rank" BETWEEN 1 AND 3
  AND DAYOFWEEK("week") BETWEEN 2 AND 6
GROUP BY "refresh_date"
ORDER BY "refresh_date" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Description: List the top three terms for each refresh_date using LISTAGG for compact view.
SELECT "refresh_date",
       LISTAGG("term", ', ') WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY "rank") AS "top_3_terms"
FROM "GOOGLE_TRENDS"."GOOGLE_TRENDS"."TOP_TERMS"
WHERE "week" BETWEEN '2024-09-01' AND '2024-09-14'
  AND "rank" BETWEEN 1 AND 3
  AND DAYOFWEEK("week") BETWEEN 2 AND 6
GROUP BY "refresh_date"
ORDER BY "refresh_date" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Description: Use ROW_NUMBER to ensure only one row per rank when duplicates might exist.
SELECT "refresh_date", "week", "term", "rank"
FROM (
      SELECT *,
             ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY "refresh_date", "rank" ORDER BY "week") AS rn
      FROM "GOOGLE_TRENDS"."GOOGLE_TRENDS"."TOP_TERMS"
      WHERE "week" BETWEEN '2024-09-01' AND '2024-09-14'
        AND "rank" BETWEEN 1 AND 3
        AND DAYOFWEEK("week") BETWEEN 2 AND 6
     )
WHERE rn = 1
ORDER BY "refresh_date" DESC, "rank"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Description: Present results grouped by refresh_date plus weekday name for clarity.
SELECT "refresh_date",
       TO_CHAR("week", 'DY') AS "weekday",
       "rank", "term"
FROM "GOOGLE_TRENDS"."GOOGLE_TRENDS"."TOP_TERMS"
WHERE "week" BETWEEN '2024-09-01' AND '2024-09-14'
  AND "rank" BETWEEN 1 AND 3
  AND DAYOFWEEK("week") BETWEEN 2 AND 6
ORDER BY "refresh_date" DESC, "rank"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. Description: Final pattern—classic GROUP BY plus LISTAGG, ordered by refresh_date descending.
SELECT "refresh_date",
       LISTAGG("term" || ' (rank=' || "rank" || ')', ', ') 
            WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY "rank") AS "ranked_top_three_terms"
FROM "GOOGLE_TRENDS"."GOOGLE_TRENDS"."TOP_TERMS"
WHERE "week" BETWEEN '2024-09-01' AND '2024-09-14'
  AND "rank" BETWEEN 1 AND 3
  AND DAYOFWEEK("week") BETWEEN 2 AND 6
GROUP BY "refresh_date"
ORDER BY "refresh_date" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

These statements progressively narrow the data and demonstrate different techniques (DISTINCT, aggregation, window functions, string concatenation) without yet providing the single consolidated answer.
